BT /F1 20 Tf 72 744 Td (Chunking Study Material) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 710 Td (Chunking means breaking a long document into smaller, meaningful units. A chapter) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 695 Td (becomes sections, a section becomes paragraphs, and a slide deck becomes individual) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 680 Td (slides. Each chunk should be small enough to finish in one focused sitting.) Tj ET
BT /F1 13 Tf 72 649 Td (Why chunking works) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 629 Td (Working memory holds only a handful of items at once. Large blocks of text overwhelm it,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 614 Td (so readers skim to cope. Short chunks reduce the load and make it easier to notice when) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 599 Td (understanding breaks down.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 576 Td (Chunk boundaries also create natural stopping points. Instead of deciding whether to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 561 Td (continue at an arbitrary moment, the reader completes a unit, pauses, and then chooses) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 546 Td (whether to go on.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 523 Td (- Small units fit working memory.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 506 Td (- Boundaries create natural breaks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 489 Td (- Completion gives a sense of progress.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 295 42 Td (Page 2) Tj ET
