BT /F1 20 Tf 72 744 Td (Retrieval Practice) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 710 Td (Retrieval practice is the act of pulling information out of memory rather than putting) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 695 Td (it in. After reading a chunk, a reader might close the text and explain the main idea) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 680 Td (aloud. This effortful recall strengthens memory far more than rereading.) Tj ET
BT /F1 13 Tf 72 649 Td (Checkpoints during reading) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 629 Td (A checkpoint is a brief pause in which the reader answers a question from memory. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 614 Td (question does not need to be graded. Its purpose is to interrupt passive reading and) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 599 Td (force the reader to reconstruct what was just learned.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 576 Td (Even a skipped checkpoint carries information. If a reader repeatedly skips or answers) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 561 Td (in a few seconds, that pattern may signal fatigue or disengagement rather than mastery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 538 Td (- Recall beats rereading.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 521 Td (- Ungraded checkpoints still improve memory.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 504 Td (- Skipping can be a signal, not just a choice.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 295 42 Td (Page 3) Tj ET
