BT /F1 20 Tf 72 744 Td (Planning a Session) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 710 Td (A good study plan starts with two numbers: how much there is to read and how much time) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 695 Td (is available. Comparing the two prevents the common mistake of setting a goal that) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 680 Td (cannot be met.) Tj ET
BT /F1 13 Tf 72 649 Td (Making the plan realistic) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 629 Td (Estimate reading time from the number of words, then add time for checkpoints. If the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 614 Td (total exceeds the available window, reduce the number of chunks rather than rushing. It) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 599 Td (is better to finish a smaller portion well than to skim a larger one.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 576 Td (A plan should also include a stopping rule. When the time is up, the reader stops and) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 561 Td (records where to resume. This makes it easier to return later without rereading the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 546 Td (whole document.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 523 Td (- Compare reading load with available time.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 506 Td (- Reduce the goal instead of rushing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 489 Td (- Record where to resume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 295 42 Td (Page 5) Tj ET
